# Operating Systems question bank
@subject Operating Systems | OS | #DC2626 | Processes, scheduling, synchronisation, deadlocks, memory and storage

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Processes & Threads

[EASY]
Q: A program in execution is called a:
A) File
B) Process
C) Compiler
D) Kernel
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which data structure does the OS keep for every process (state, registers, PID, open files)?
A) Page table only
B) Process Control Block (PCB)
C) File allocation table
D) Inode
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: A process in the READY state is waiting for:
A) I/O to finish
B) The CPU
C) A signal from the user
D) Termination
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which system call creates a new process in Unix/Linux?
A) exec()
B) fork()
C) wait()
D) exit()
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which of these is shared between threads of the same process?
A) Stack
B) Program counter
C) Registers
D) Heap memory and global variables
ANSWER: D

[MEDIUM]
Q: How many lines does this C program print?
CODE c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int main(void) {
    fork();
    fork();
    fork();
    printf("hello\n");
    while (wait(NULL) > 0);
    return 0;
}
END
A) 8
B) 3
C) 6
D) 4
ANSWER: A
HINT: Each fork() doubles the number of processes that run the following code.
EXPLAIN: After n forks there are 2^n processes: 2^3 = 8, and each prints once.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this C program?
CODE c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int x = 10;

int main(void) {
    pid_t pid = fork();
    if (pid == 0) {
        x += 5;
        printf("child %d\n", x);
        return 0;
    }
    wait(NULL);
    x -= 5;
    printf("parent %d\n", x);
    return 0;
}
END
A) child 15 parent 5
B) child 15 parent 10
C) child 15 parent 15
D) parent 5 child 15
ANSWER: A
HINT: After fork() the child gets its own COPY of the parent's memory. wait() makes the parent print last.
EXPLAIN: The child changes its copy to 15; the parent's x is still 10 and becomes 5.

[MEDIUM]
Q: Which statement about a context switch is correct?
A) The OS saves the state (registers, PC) of the running process in its PCB and loads the state of the next process
B) It copies the whole process memory to disk every time
C) It happens only when a process terminates
D) It requires a reboot
ANSWER: A
HINT: Think about what must be remembered to resume a process exactly where it stopped.
EXPLAIN: The switch itself does no useful work, so it is pure overhead; switching threads of the same process is cheaper because the address space stays.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this C program?
CODE c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>

int main(void) {
    int n = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        if (fork() == 0) {
            n++;
        } else {
            wait(NULL);
        }
    }
    printf("%d ", n);
    fflush(stdout);
    return 0;
}
END
A) 2 1 1 0
B) 0 1 1 2
C) 1 1 1 1
D) 2 2 1 0
ANSWER: A
HINT: Draw the process tree. Every parent waits for its child before continuing the loop or printing.
EXPLAIN: The deepest child (two increments) prints first: 2; then its parent (1); then the second child of the original (1); finally the original process (0).

[HARD]
Q: A zombie and an orphan process differ in that:
A) A zombie has finished but its parent has not called wait(); an orphan is still running but its parent has exited (it is adopted by init/systemd)
B) A zombie is still running; an orphan has finished
C) Both are the same
D) An orphan is a process without a PCB
ANSWER: A
HINT: Ask "who has terminated: the child or the parent?"
EXPLAIN: Zombies hold only a process-table entry until reaped; orphans are re-parented to PID 1, which reaps them when they finish.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic CPU Scheduling

[EASY]
Q: Which scheduling algorithm runs processes in the order they arrive?
A) FCFS (First Come First Served)
B) SJF
C) Round Robin
D) Priority
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Round Robin scheduling gives each process a fixed:
A) Priority
B) Time quantum (time slice)
C) Memory block
D) Number of instructions
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which algorithm gives the minimum average waiting time for a given set of processes (all available at time 0)?
A) FCFS
B) Shortest Job First
C) Round Robin
D) Longest Job First
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Starvation in priority scheduling can be solved by:
A) Aging (gradually increasing the priority of waiting processes)
B) Increasing the time quantum
C) Using more memory
D) Killing low-priority processes
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Turnaround time of a process is:
A) Completion time - arrival time
B) Burst time - waiting time
C) Arrival time - start time
D) The time slice
ANSWER: A
EXPLAIN: Waiting time = turnaround time - burst time.

[MEDIUM]
Q: Processes P1, P2, P3 arrive at time 0 with burst times 24, 3 and 3 and are scheduled FCFS in that order. What is the average waiting time?
A) 17
B) 3
C) 10
D) 27
ANSWER: A
HINT: Waiting time of each process = the time it starts (all arrive at 0).
EXPLAIN: P1 waits 0, P2 waits 24, P3 waits 27: (0 + 24 + 27) / 3 = 17. Running the short jobs first (SJF) would give 3.

[MEDIUM]
Q: Processes P1 (burst 5), P2 (burst 3) and P3 (burst 1) all arrive at time 0. With Round Robin and quantum 2 (order P1, P2, P3), at what time does P1 finish?
A) 9
B) 7
C) 5
D) 8
ANSWER: A
HINT: Write the Gantt chart: P1 0-2, P2 2-4, P3 4-5, then continue the cycle.
EXPLAIN: P1 0-2, P2 2-4, P3 4-5 (done), P1 5-7, P2 7-8 (done), P1 8-9 (done).

[MEDIUM]
Q: What does this Python simulation of SJF (non-preemptive, all arriving at 0) print?
CODE python
bursts = {"P1": 6, "P2": 8, "P3": 7, "P4": 3}
t, waits = 0, {}
for p, b in sorted(bursts.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1]):
    waits[p] = t
    t += b
print(sum(waits.values()) / len(waits))
END
A) 7.0
B) 10.25
C) 6.0
D) 8.0
ANSWER: A
HINT: Order by burst: P4 (3), P1 (6), P3 (7), P2 (8). Each process waits for the bursts before it.
EXPLAIN: Waits are 0, 3, 9, 16, average 28 / 4 = 7.0.

[HARD]
Q: Using preemptive SJF (SRTF): P1 (arrival 0, burst 8), P2 (arrival 1, burst 4), P3 (arrival 2, burst 9), P4 (arrival 3, burst 5). What is the average waiting time?
A) 6.5
B) 7.75
C) 5.75
D) 8.0
ANSWER: A
HINT: At each arrival compare the new burst with the REMAINING time of the running process. Gantt: P1 0-1, P2 1-5, P4 5-10, P1 10-17, P3 17-26.
EXPLAIN: Waiting = finish - arrival - burst: P1 17-0-8 = 9, P2 5-1-4 = 0, P3 26-2-9 = 15, P4 10-3-5 = 2. (9 + 0 + 15 + 2) / 4 = 6.5.

[HARD]
Q: In Round Robin, what happens as the time quantum becomes very large, and as it becomes very small?
A) Very large: behaves like FCFS; very small: context-switch overhead dominates
B) Very large: behaves like SJF; very small: behaves like FCFS
C) Quantum size never affects performance
D) Very large: starvation of all processes; very small: deadlock
ANSWER: A
HINT: Consider a quantum longer than every burst, and a quantum close to the context-switch time.
EXPLAIN: A quantum longer than all bursts means no preemption (FCFS). A tiny quantum causes so many switches that little useful work is done.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Synchronization

[EASY]
Q: The part of a program that accesses shared data and must not be executed by two processes at once is the:
A) Critical section
B) Kernel
C) Interrupt handler
D) Stack frame
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: A semaphore is mainly used for:
A) Memory allocation
B) Process/thread synchronisation
C) File compression
D) Disk formatting
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: A binary semaphore can take which values?
A) Any integer
B) Only 0 and 1
C) Only negative values
D) 0 to 255
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: A race condition happens when:
A) The result depends on the unpredictable order in which threads access shared data
B) Two programs use the same CPU
C) A process runs too fast
D) The disk is full
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Which three requirements must a correct critical-section solution satisfy?
A) Mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting
B) Speed, safety and size
C) Paging, segmentation and swapping
D) Fork, exec and wait
ANSWER: A

[MEDIUM]
Q: A counting semaphore S is initialised to 3. Five processes each call wait(S) (P operation) without any signal. How many processes are blocked?
A) 2
B) 3
C) 5
D) 0
ANSWER: A
HINT: Each wait decrements S; a process blocks when no units remain.
EXPLAIN: The first three get a unit (S reaches 0); the remaining two block (S conceptually -2).

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this C program?
CODE c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>

int counter = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void *work(void *arg) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
        counter++;
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main(void) {
    pthread_t t1, t2;
    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, work, NULL);
    pthread_create(&t2, NULL, work, NULL);
    pthread_join(t1, NULL);
    pthread_join(t2, NULL);
    printf("%d\n", counter);
    return 0;
}
END
A) 200000
B) A random number below 200000
C) 100000
D) 0
ANSWER: A
HINT: Is every increment protected, and does main wait for both threads?
EXPLAIN: The mutex makes counter++ mutually exclusive and pthread_join waits for both threads, so no update is lost.

[MEDIUM]
Q: In the producer-consumer problem with a bounded buffer of size N, which semaphores are typically used?
A) mutex = 1, empty = N, full = 0
B) mutex = 0, empty = 0, full = N
C) mutex = N, empty = 1, full = 1
D) Only one semaphore initialised to N
ANSWER: A
HINT: Producers wait for empty slots, consumers wait for full slots, and both need exclusive buffer access.
EXPLAIN: empty counts free slots (starts at N), full counts filled slots (starts at 0), and mutex protects the buffer itself.

[HARD]
Q: Peterson's solution for two processes uses flag[2] and turn. Process i runs: flag[i] = true; turn = j; while (flag[j] && turn == j); /* critical section */ flag[i] = false. Why is turn set to the OTHER process?
A) To be polite: if both want to enter, the one that wrote turn last waits, guaranteeing mutual exclusion without deadlock
B) To make process j enter twice
C) Because turn must always equal j
D) To cause starvation deliberately
ANSWER: A
HINT: Consider both processes setting their flags at the same time; only one value of turn survives.
EXPLAIN: The last writer of turn gives way, so exactly one process proceeds (mutual exclusion), and the other enters next (progress and bounded waiting).

[HARD]
Q: In the producer-consumer solution, what goes wrong if the producer calls wait(mutex) BEFORE wait(empty)?
A) Deadlock: when the buffer is full the producer sleeps on empty while holding the mutex, so the consumer can never enter to free a slot
B) Nothing; the order does not matter
C) Items are produced twice
D) The consumer reads an empty buffer
ANSWER: A
HINT: What does the consumer need to acquire before it can signal empty?
EXPLAIN: The producer blocks inside the critical section holding mutex; the consumer blocks on mutex and can never signal empty. Always wait on the counting semaphore first.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Deadlocks

[EASY]
Q: Which of these is NOT one of the four necessary conditions for deadlock?
A) Mutual exclusion
B) Hold and wait
C) Preemption
D) Circular wait
ANSWER: C
EXPLAIN: The condition is NO preemption.

[EASY]
Q: The Banker's algorithm is used for:
A) Deadlock avoidance
B) Page replacement
C) Disk scheduling
D) CPU scheduling
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: A resource allocation graph with a cycle, where every resource has a single instance, means:
A) A deadlock exists
B) There is no deadlock
C) The system is idle
D) Memory is full
ANSWER: A
EXPLAIN: With multiple instances per resource, a cycle means deadlock is possible but not certain.

[EASY]
Q: Requiring processes to request resources in a fixed global order prevents which condition?
A) Mutual exclusion
B) Circular wait
C) No preemption
D) Hold and wait
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Restarting the computer or killing processes to break a deadlock is part of:
A) Deadlock recovery
B) Deadlock prevention
C) Deadlock avoidance
D) Scheduling
ANSWER: A

[MEDIUM]
Q: A system has 12 tape drives and 3 processes with maximum needs P0 = 10, P1 = 4, P2 = 9. Currently P0 holds 5, P1 holds 2, P2 holds 2. Is the state safe?
A) Yes, safe sequence P1, P0, P2
B) No, it is unsafe
C) Yes, safe sequence P0, P1, P2
D) Yes, safe sequence P2, P0, P1
ANSWER: A
HINT: Available = 12 - (5 + 2 + 2) = 3. Find a process whose remaining need fits in 3.
EXPLAIN: P1 needs 2 (≤3) -> available 5; P0 needs 5 -> available 10; P2 needs 7 -> done. Safe sequence P1, P0, P2.

[MEDIUM]
Q: Five dining philosophers each pick up the left fork then the right fork. Which simple change prevents deadlock?
A) Let one philosopher pick up the right fork first (break the circular wait)
B) Add more food
C) Let every philosopher pick up the left fork twice
D) Give each philosopher a higher priority
ANSWER: A
HINT: Deadlock needs every philosopher to hold one fork and wait for the next in a circle.
EXPLAIN: Making one philosopher asymmetric breaks the cycle; another option is allowing at most four philosophers to sit at once.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What does this Python Banker's safety check print?
CODE python
avail = [3, 3, 2]
alloc = {"P0": [0, 1, 0], "P1": [2, 0, 0], "P2": [3, 0, 2], "P3": [2, 1, 1], "P4": [0, 0, 2]}
need  = {"P0": [7, 4, 3], "P1": [1, 2, 2], "P2": [6, 0, 0], "P3": [0, 1, 1], "P4": [4, 3, 1]}
order, done = [], set()
while len(done) < len(alloc):
    for p in alloc:
        if p not in done and all(n <= a for n, a in zip(need[p], avail)):
            avail = [a + x for a, x in zip(avail, alloc[p])]
            done.add(p)
            order.append(p)
            break
    else:
        break
print(order)
END
A) ['P1', 'P3', 'P0', 'P2', 'P4']
B) ['P1', 'P3', 'P4', 'P0', 'P2']
C) ['P0', 'P1', 'P2', 'P3', 'P4']
D) []
ANSWER: A
HINT: Each round restarts the scan from P0 and takes the FIRST process whose need fits the available vector.
EXPLAIN: avail 332 -> P1 fits (532) -> P3 fits (743) -> rescan: P0 (743) fits (753) -> P2 (10 5 5) -> P4. The state is safe.

[HARD]
Q: A system has 3 identical resources shared by processes that each need at most 2. What is the maximum number of processes for which deadlock is impossible?
A) 2
B) 3
C) 1
D) 4
ANSWER: A
HINT: Worst case: every process holds (max - 1) resources. Deadlock is impossible if one extra resource is still free.
EXPLAIN: With n processes holding 1 each, n + 1 ≤ 3 must hold, so n ≤ 2. With 3 processes each holding one, all wait forever.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this C program (it acquires locks in a consistent order)?
CODE c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>

pthread_mutex_t a = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER, b = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
int total = 0;

void *t1(void *x) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&a); pthread_mutex_lock(&b);
        total += 1;
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&b); pthread_mutex_unlock(&a);
    }
    return NULL;
}
void *t2(void *x) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&a); pthread_mutex_lock(&b);
        total += 2;
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&b); pthread_mutex_unlock(&a);
    }
    return NULL;
}

int main(void) {
    pthread_t x, y;
    pthread_create(&x, NULL, t1, NULL);
    pthread_create(&y, NULL, t2, NULL);
    pthread_join(x, NULL);
    pthread_join(y, NULL);
    printf("%d\n", total);
    return 0;
}
END
A) 3000
B) The program deadlocks
C) A value below 3000
D) 2000
ANSWER: A
HINT: Deadlock needs a circular wait. Do the two threads ever lock a and b in opposite orders?
EXPLAIN: Both threads lock a then b, so no cycle can form. If t2 locked b first, the program could deadlock.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Memory Management

[EASY]
Q: What is the main purpose of virtual memory?
A) Increase CPU speed
B) Let programs use more memory than physically installed by using disk as backing store
C) Encrypt memory
D) Reduce disk usage
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Paging divides physical memory into fixed-size blocks called:
A) Pages
B) Frames
C) Segments
D) Sectors
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: Logical memory is divided into pages of the same size as the frames.

[EASY]
Q: A page fault occurs when:
A) A program accesses a page that is not currently in main memory
B) The CPU overheats
C) A page is printed
D) A file is deleted
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Excessive paging that leaves the CPU mostly idle is called:
A) Thrashing
B) Swapping
C) Caching
D) Spooling
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: The TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer) is a:
A) Fast cache of recent page-table entries
B) Type of hard disk
C) Scheduling queue
D) File system table
ANSWER: A

[MEDIUM]
Q: With FIFO page replacement and 3 frames, how many page faults occur for the reference string 7 0 1 2 0 3 0 4?
A) 7
B) 6
C) 8
D) 5
ANSWER: A
HINT: Track the three frames; on a fault replace the page that has been in memory the longest.
EXPLAIN: Faults on 7, 0, 1, 2 (replaces 7), 3 (replaces 0), 0 (replaces 1), 4 (replaces 2). The second 0 is a hit. Total 7.

[MEDIUM]
Q: A system uses 32-bit logical addresses and 4 KB pages. How many entries does a single-level page table have?
A) 2^20 (about 1 million)
B) 2^12
C) 2^32
D) 2^10
ANSWER: A
HINT: Offset bits = log2(4096). The remaining bits form the page number.
EXPLAIN: 12 bits of offset leave 20 bits for the page number, so 2^20 entries.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What does this Python LRU simulation print?
CODE python
def lru_faults(refs, frames):
    mem, faults = [], 0
    for p in refs:
        if p in mem:
            mem.remove(p)
        else:
            faults += 1
            if len(mem) == frames:
                mem.pop(0)
        mem.append(p)
    return faults

print(lru_faults([7, 0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 0, 4, 2, 3, 0, 3, 2], 3))
END
A) 9
B) 10
C) 8
D) 7
ANSWER: A
HINT: The list keeps pages in order of last use; index 0 is the least recently used page.
EXPLAIN: Faults on 7, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 2, 3, 0 = 9; the hits are 0, 0, 3 and 2.

[HARD]
Q: Effective memory access time: memory access 100 ns, TLB lookup 20 ns, TLB hit ratio 80%. With a single-level page table, what is the effective access time?
A) 140 ns
B) 120 ns
C) 220 ns
D) 100 ns
ANSWER: A
HINT: A hit costs TLB + one memory access; a miss costs TLB + page-table access + memory access.
EXPLAIN: 0.8 x (20 + 100) + 0.2 x (20 + 100 + 100) = 96 + 44 = 140 ns.

[HARD]
Q: Belady's anomaly means:
A) With FIFO replacement, increasing the number of frames can INCREASE the number of page faults
B) LRU always gives more faults than FIFO
C) Optimal replacement can be implemented in practice
D) More frames always reduce faults for every algorithm
ANSWER: A
HINT: The classic example is the reference string 1 2 3 4 1 2 5 1 2 3 4 5 with 3 and 4 frames.
EXPLAIN: FIFO gives 9 faults with 3 frames but 10 with 4. Stack algorithms such as LRU and Optimal never show this anomaly.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic File Systems & Disk Scheduling

[EASY]
Q: In Unix-like file systems, the metadata of a file (size, owner, permissions, block pointers) is stored in an:
A) Inode
B) Directory only
C) Boot block
D) Page table
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Which disk scheduling algorithm serves the request closest to the current head position next?
A) FCFS
B) SSTF (Shortest Seek Time First)
C) SCAN
D) LOOK
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which allocation method stores each file in consecutive disk blocks?
A) Contiguous allocation
B) Linked allocation
C) Indexed allocation
D) Random allocation
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: The SCAN disk scheduling algorithm is also known as the:
A) Elevator algorithm
B) Round robin algorithm
C) Banker's algorithm
D) Clock algorithm
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Which RAID level uses mirroring (an exact copy on a second disk)?
A) RAID 0
B) RAID 1
C) RAID 5
D) RAID 6
ANSWER: B

[MEDIUM]
Q: The disk head is at cylinder 50. Requests: 82, 170, 43, 140, 24, 16, 190. What is the total head movement using FCFS?
A) 642
B) 208
C) 332
D) 500
ANSWER: A
HINT: Add the absolute differences in the given order, starting from 50.
EXPLAIN: 32 + 88 + 127 + 97 + 116 + 8 + 174 = 642.

[MEDIUM]
Q: Same requests (head at 50): 82, 170, 43, 140, 24, 16, 190. What is the total head movement using SSTF?
A) 208
B) 642
C) 332
D) 226
ANSWER: A
HINT: From 50 the nearest request is 43. Keep choosing the nearest remaining request.
EXPLAIN: 50->43->24->16 (34), then 16->82->140->170->190 (174): total 208.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What does this Python code print (total head movement for LOOK, moving towards larger cylinders first)?
CODE python
head, reqs = 50, [82, 170, 43, 140, 24, 16, 190]
up = sorted(r for r in reqs if r >= head)
down = sorted((r for r in reqs if r < head), reverse=True)
total, pos = 0, head
for r in up + down:
    total += abs(r - pos)
    pos = r
print(total)
END
A) 314
B) 208
C) 332
D) 642
ANSWER: A
HINT: LOOK goes up to the last request (190), then reverses to the lowest request (16).
EXPLAIN: 50 -> 190 is 140, then 190 -> 16 is 174: total 314. SCAN would travel to the disk end (199) first.

[HARD]
Q: A Unix inode has 12 direct pointers, one single-indirect and one double-indirect pointer. Blocks are 4 KB and each pointer is 4 bytes. What is the largest file size possible (ignoring triple indirect)?
A) About 4 GB (48 KB + 4 MB + 4 GB)
B) 48 KB
C) About 4 MB
D) About 16 GB
ANSWER: A
HINT: One 4 KB block holds 4096 / 4 = 1024 pointers.
EXPLAIN: Direct: 12 x 4 KB = 48 KB; single: 1024 x 4 KB = 4 MB; double: 1024 x 1024 x 4 KB = 4 GB.

[HARD]
Q: RAID 5 with 5 disks of 2 TB each provides how much usable capacity, and how many disk failures can it survive?
A) 8 TB; one disk failure
B) 10 TB; no failures
C) 5 TB; two failures
D) 8 TB; two failures
ANSWER: A
HINT: RAID 5 spreads parity equal to one disk's capacity across all disks.
EXPLAIN: (n - 1) x 2 TB = 8 TB usable. Single distributed parity lets it rebuild from one failed disk; RAID 6 tolerates two.
